WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.820
Video analysis consumes resources in several stages: network input, codec decode, frame conversion, inference, event logic, encoding, storage, and user access.

2
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.600
Measure the entire pipeline.

3
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.580
A GPU may be idle while CPU decode or storage becomes the bottleneck.

4
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.860
CPUs can support modest frame sampling and lightweight models.

5
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.360
GPUs provide high parallel throughput for detection and larger models.

6
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:34.300
NPUs and dedicated accelerators can offer efficient inference for supported model formats.

7
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.840
Hardware compatibility matters as much as advertised speed.

8
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:43.400
Consumer GPUs can be cost-effective but have fixed memory and operational limits.

9
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.100
Mini PCs and edge appliances suit smaller sites.

10
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:53.760
Apple Silicon offers integrated CPU, GPU, and media capabilities for compatible software.

11
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:59.540
Local servers provide more expansion, cooling, storage, and redundancy options.

12
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:06.780
Model size, input resolution, precision, batch size, and sample rate determine inference demand.

13
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.840
Batching can improve throughput while adding delay.

14
00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:15.220
Event-triggered sampling can reduce cost but may miss activity if the trigger is weak.

15
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:25.180
Capacity plans should cover average and peak streams, concurrent events, dashboard use, model reloads, storage jobs, and hardware failure.

16
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.360
Preserve headroom rather than sizing to a clean benchmark.

17
00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.740
Power, cooling, noise, and physical placement also affect reliability.

18
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:40.140
Test with representative cameras, codecs, lighting, models, and alert rules.

19
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:46.960
Track latency, dropped frames, queue depth, memory, temperature, and event completion.

20
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:53.620
Match hardware to required service levels, then define what the system should degrade or postpone when resources are exhausted.
